{
  "fromId": "sh_retroflex",
  "toId": "sh",
  "preamble": "The voiceless retroflex fricative [ʂ] loses its retroflexion and becomes the postalveolar [ʃ]. Proto-Quechuan *ʂ became [ʃ] across Quechua I and in several Quechua II varieties, and the Proto-Kartvelian sibilant *s₁ gave š [ʃ] in Mingrelian, Laz and Svan. The [ʃ] heard for ṣ in Sanskrit loanwords in Hindi, Bengali and other modern Indo-Aryan languages is a learned pronunciation, not an inherited change, and is not listed here. Southern and Taiwan Mandarin deretroflexion goes to [s], not [ʃ] (see sh_retroflex_to_s).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Deretroflexion, Merger",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Quechua I (Huaylas, Huánuco, Huanca, Pacaraos)",
      "languageFamily": "Quechuan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Quechuan *iʂkaj 'two'",
          "to": "iʃkaj",
          "note": "The same [ʃ] reflex appears in Cajamarca, Ecuadorian (Imbabura iʃki) and Santiagueño Quechua, while Ayacucho, Cuzco and Bolivian Quechua have iskaj."
        }
      ]
    },
